import {BEATS} from './content.js';

const add=(id,html)=>{BEATS[id].body+=html};

add('s1-waking',`<p>Someone had turned in the night and left a knee against Kalos’s back. Someone else had stolen the soft edge of the sleeping mat. The whole house made one animal of its heat and still managed to leave Pali cold.</p><p>Kalos knew the predawn sounds well enough to sort them without opening his eyes: Eda scraping yesterday’s fat from a stone bowl; Veya clearing her throat before she said something no one wanted to hear; the outer door lifting and settling in wind. Ruvan had gone before the moon lowered. His empty place smelled of wet cedar and the pitch he used on canoe seams.</p><p>Seli tied her hair without a mirror. She had begun doing things that way lately—cleanly, silently, as if competence were a path adults might fail to block if she stepped onto it before they woke.</p>`);
add('s1-hearth',`<p>Eda’s work looked easy only when she was doing it. She knew which fish had been opened first, which oil jar belonged to a visiting aunt, and how much generosity the household could display before generosity became a winter problem. Kalos knew none of that. He knew the large piece had bubbled at one edge and that Seli’s mouth had tightened when she smelled it.</p><p>The visitor near Veya wore a rain cloak with the shoulders darkened almost black. A little water had gathered beneath him on the floor. He kept his hands open while he spoke, which meant either that he meant no harm or that he wanted them to notice he carried nothing worth taking.</p><p>Pali shifted under the blanket. If he woke fully, he would call first for Eda and then, if she did not answer, for Kalos. The order pleased Kalos more than he would have admitted even to himself.</p>`);
add('s1-road',`<p>The path ran between houses before it fell toward the mud. Smoke from one roof joined smoke from another until no child could have said which household had made which part of the morning sky.</p><p>At the lower turn, two women were arguing about a basket of mussels without raising their voices. A dog watched them with more interest than either woman showed the dog. Farther out, figures bent over a canoe that had come home with one side scraped pale.</p><p>Kalos wanted to know all of it. He also wanted to arrive where the older children were already laughing. The path narrowed exactly where wanting several things became a need to put one body beside one person.</p>`);
add('s2-lesson',`<p>The hooks were made from bone cut thin and polished until the curve looked grown rather than carved. Kalos liked them before the fiber went on. Each was a small white question whose answer was supposed to be a fish.</p><p>Wet binding fiber had a green smell under the heavier air of oil and drying flesh. It resisted the first pull, then yielded too suddenly. Mara’s hands accounted for the change before it happened. Neri’s did too, though his fingers were only a little longer than Kalos’s.</p><p>Oren’s basket passed behind them once more. This time no adult was near enough to justify the journey. Neri’s eyes lifted for the length of one breath and returned to the hook. Kalos understood the invitation to laugh and the invitation not to. They occupied the same glance.</p>`);
add('s2-interruption',`<p>Pali’s face had not yet decided what had happened. His mouth opened, but no sound came. For that suspended instant he could still be persuaded that the fall was funny, or frightening, or someone else’s fault.</p><p>Mara’s tray slid toward the dark water spreading across the mat. Neri’s grip whitened around the bowl. Seli was nowhere in sight. Kalos saw all four facts at once and could answer only one of them with his hands.</p><p>Behind Oren, a man at the cleaning stones turned at the sound. Oren’s laugh changed when he noticed. It grew shorter and more useful, the laugh of an older child who had been observing an accident rather than enjoying it.</p>`);
add('s2-assignment',`<p>Mara selected the hooks instead of taking the next eight. Two had narrow shanks that punished loose wrapping. One had a rough place near the point. She was not giving Kalos children’s work. She was giving him work that would reveal the kind of child doing it.</p><p>The separate tray pleased him. It made a boundary around his responsibility and, for a moment, around his importance. People stepping through the yard could see that these eight belonged to him.</p><p>Then he counted the distance to sunset: the angle of light beneath the rack, the water climbing the mud, the voices of children who would be released from their own work sooner than he would. Eight became larger when measured against an afternoon.</p>`);
add('s3-call',`<p>Kalos had been good for most of an hour. Good in the unobserved, repetitive way that did not feel like a story anyone would tell. The first six hooks had accumulated without incident. The seventh required repair and survived it.</p><p>The eighth was the rough one Mara had chosen. A ridge in the bone lifted the fiber unless he pressed each turn down with his thumbnail. He had begun correctly. He could see the correct ending from where he knelt.</p><p>Oren called again, this time using the secret name the older children had given Kalos after he made the seal-hunter story better. Neri heard it. Kalos felt the name travel through him like food.</p><p>Beyond the racks, someone struck the black post and shouted that the race had begun. The hook did not know.</p>`);
add('s3-race',`<p>The drainage cut was not deep. A fall would bruise a hip, cover a child in black mud, and live in other children’s mouths for weeks. That was enough danger for a game.</p><p>Oren stood on the dry side of the beam when he announced the new rule. He had not crossed it himself. One of the younger girls objected, but quietly, with the hopelessness of someone stating weather.</p><p>Neri scraped drying mud from one ankle. He could leave without losing much standing because he had little the game recognized. Kalos could leave too, but his absence would have shape. The others would notice the hollow where his joke or challenge ought to have been.</p><p>Wind turned the hanging fish all at once. Their silvered skins flashed above the children like a signal no one understood.</p>`);
add('s3-laugh',`<p>Neri’s fall had not hurt him. That fact did not make the laughter harmless, though it gave everyone permission to pretend it did.</p><p>His knees were black to the hem. A thin line of mud had reached his cheek. He wiped it with the clean back of his wrist and made the mark larger. Oren saw and stored the movement for another imitation.</p><p>Kalos had learned, before he knew he was learning, that a group rarely chose the best joke by itself. It waited for someone to show where the pleasure was allowed to gather. Sometimes the showing was generous. Sometimes it merely made the choice look inevitable.</p><p>Neri did not look at Oren. He looked at Kalos, which placed a kind of weight in Kalos’s mouth before he had spoken.</p>`);
add('s4-return',`<p>The yard at sunset belonged to unfinished things. Knives waited beside fish that had not been opened. A half-scraped hide darkened where someone had poured water over it. Oren’s basket leaned against a post with its weak corner turned inward.</p><p>Kalos’s tray seemed smaller than it had at midday. The hooks no longer marked him as important. They marked only what had happened while no one watched.</p><p>Mara’s question was ordinary. She had asked similar questions all day and would ask more before sleeping. That made it harder, not easier. Kalos could not persuade himself that the entire household had arranged the moment to test him. It had merely reached the place where an answer was needed.</p><p>At the shore, the older children were deciding whether there was enough light for one final game.</p>`);
add('s4-morning-clear',`<p>The line left a pale groove across Mara’s palms. She inspected the hook for movement and found none.</p><p>Kalos had imagined, while finishing it, that success might contain a sensation equal to the temptation it defeated. It did not. The hook held. The adults used it. The tide continued to leave.</p><p>That ordinariness felt almost insulting. He had given up the first race, and the morning had no knowledge of what it owed him. Neri’s single glance was the nearest thing to witness.</p><p>Oren knelt over his basket with two adults correcting different parts of it at once. His face had become blank with the effort not to show humiliation. Kalos could make the morning notice one of them. He could also let it remain useful.</p>`);
add('s4-accusation',`<p>Kalos knew the binding before Mara turned it toward the light. He knew the place where his thumbnail had stopped pressing. He knew the two turns laid over each other because Oren had called his secret name from the beach.</p><p>Neri could not know those things. He knew only that the accusation had found him by the shortest path: he had carried the tray; he belonged to a household adults trusted less; he was serious enough that guilty children looked more innocent beside him.</p><p>Oren’s certainty was partly performance and partly relief. His own basket had failed at the corner. If Mara spent the morning examining Neri, she might not examine him.</p><p>Kalos could feel the joke about the basket complete itself. It was good. That was one of the difficulties.</p>`);
add('s5-mara',`<p>The shed was close enough to the water that high tides spoke beneath it. At low tide the voice withdrew and left smells behind.</p><p>Mara divided a length of fiber into six equal bundles. She did not ask Kalos to promise anything. That omission stung more cleanly than anger would have.</p><p>He could still feel the public confession in his body: heat behind the ears, looseness in the legs, the strange relief after the words could no longer be taken back. The feeling made him want credit for having endured it. Mara gave him none.</p><p>She placed her first turn on the hook. Kalos watched, though he already knew how. Outside, the unseen joke reached its laugh.</p>`);
add('s5-evidence',`<p>By the fourth test, the fishing party had stopped pretending not to listen. An adult in the canoe rested both hands on his paddle. The pause was now costing everyone, and delay itself began to look like wrongdoing.</p><p>Neri’s face changed less than Oren’s. Oren lost certainty in pieces: first the forward lean, then the narrowed eyes, then the expression of helpful concern he had prepared for Mara.</p><p>Kalos had wanted evidence because evidence did not require courage. Now the evidence had created a sharper question. It had cleared Neri’s hands without explaining Kalos’s mouth.</p><p>Mara set the fourth hook beside the first three. She did not repeat her question. Silence, in her hands, became a tool as exact as the fiber.</p>`);
add('s5-neri',`<p>Neri had chosen the place behind the shed because adults rarely came there while the tide was out. It was not private—the beach voices carried clearly—but privacy for children often meant only that no adult was looking directly at them.</p><p>The failed hook rested on a piece of old mat. Neri had already removed Kalos’s binding. The loose turns lay curled like dead insects.</p><p>Kalos could sit down and make the work faster. He could make the truth public and alter the whole morning. He could bargain because Neri had less power to refuse a bargain than Kalos did to offer one. He could deny what both boys knew.</p><p>From the beach came a shout for Kalos. Not Oren this time. Several children together. The call made leaving feel less like abandonment and more like being wanted.</p>`);
add('s5-prevention',`<p>Mara’s next task was to separate usable fiber from strands that had dried with hidden splits. It required less skill than binding hooks and more attention than anyone would praise.</p><p>Kalos ran each strand between finger and thumb. A flaw announced itself as softness before it became visible. Neri worked from the opposite end of the bundle. Their hands approached and retreated without touching.</p><p>Oren opened the failed corner of his basket. Every correction made the weave uglier before it made it sound. He looked once toward Kalos, expecting either help or pleasure. Kalos was not certain which expectation gave him more power.</p><p>The canoe became small on the gray water. No one in it knew that Kalos had prevented a bad hook from reaching them. Their ignorance was complete and reasonable.</p>`);
add('s6-close',`<p>Rain changed the size of the household. Work moved indoors. Tempers occupied more space. Wet garments hung where children had run. The visitor from the fog-damaged racks remained two nights longer than anyone first intended, and his presence altered who ate beside whom.</p><p>Mara asked to see Kalos’s work now instead of asking whether it was finished. Neri sometimes chose another place to sit. Or he chose Kalos’s place and made Kalos decide whether to understand the invitation. Oren’s story improved each time facts left it.</p><p>Kalos did not think of the hook every day. Children cannot live inside one lesson as adults later imagine they did. He thought about food, games, his father’s return, the sound Pali made when coughing, and whether rain would make the drainage cut deep enough for a more dangerous race.</p><p>Still, when someone asked whether a task was done, a small part of the drying yard returned: gray light, bone in Mara’s hand, Neri looking without pleading. The memory did not tell him what to do. It merely arrived before he did it.</p>`);

await import('./app.js');
